Fault injection and resilience and System Reliability

Fault injection, steady-state hypotheses, budget invariants.

Runner: `scripts/fault/test_resilience.sh`. CI: `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md`.

Principles

Each experiment follows: define steady state -> hypothesize stability -> inject fault -> compare metrics -> abort on breach.

eSPX steady state defaults:

- `/track` RPS stable without unexplained drops
- Latency p95 < 50 ms, p99 < 80 ms, hard ceiling 100 ms
- Error rate < 0.1% (excluding valid filter rejects)
- Budget drift: Redis spend + sync deltas ~= Postgres within recon window

Measure baseline before fault injection. Limit blast radius: one shard, one campaign, circuit breakers.

Requirements

R1. Steady-state hypothesis

Document target metrics before running. Abort if steady state degrades beyond limit.

R2. Real-world fault injection

Simulate failures in docker-compose: SIGKILL on Redis/Postgres, partitions, latency, CPU throttle, clock drift, DB unavailable.

Unit mocks alone are not sufficient proof for merge. Validate under compose with OpenRTB traffic before release.

R3. Blast radius isolation

Client sharding limits Redis outage to ~1/N campaigns. Circuit breakers on tracker, processor, edge. CI aborts on invariant failure.

R4. Distributed invariants

- Idempotency on all writes: `idempotency:click:{click_id}` in Lua + `sync_idempotency` in Postgres; CH `insert_deduplicate=1`.
- Hot-path timeouts use monotonic time (`nanotime`), not wall clock.
- Fencing tokens for distributed writes; stale leaders rejected at storage.
- Crash-recovery model; validate payloads at boundary (Nginx DFA) before gnet parse.

R5. Integration test standards

Forbidden: mocks for budget logic, click dedup, or Lua in integration tests.

- Real Redis and Postgres via `testcontainers-go`
- >= 20 concurrent goroutines on balance/limit paths
- Hot path: 0 allocs/op (`go test -benchmem`)
- Automatic recovery after dependency restore
- Post-test: `Sum Redis spend + sync deltas = Postgres spend` (micro-unit tolerance)

R6. Testing pyramid

Unit: Tables, BCE, Lua, 0 allocs
Smoke: `check_deps.sh`, `smoke_local.sh`, `verify_redis_topology.sh`
E2E: Nginx -> tracker -> Redis -> PG/CH
Fault: `TestFault_*`, compose scenarios, `fault_proof` lines

R7. `fault_proof` protocol

Passing fault tests print:

```text
fault_proof fault=<fault_name> <key>=<value> ...
```

`scripts/fault/test_resilience.sh` counts unique proofs. Default `RESILIENCE_MIN_PROOFS=52` (`RESILIENCE_MIN_PROOFS` env). Build fails below threshold.

R8. Observability during fault

`ad_tracker_health_degraded`: 1 while shard down
`ad_redis_breaker_state`: 0 closed, 1 half-open, 2 open
`ad_redis_lua_noscript_total`: Spike on deploy / `SCRIPT FLUSH`
`ad_processor_stream_lag_seconds`: Bounded post-recovery
`ad_management_outbox_oldest_pending_seconds`: Alert if > 30 s

Alert thresholds: p99 > 80 ms for 30 s, breaker open > 5 min, DLQ > 100.

R9. Experiment design

Document: hypothesis, control group, fault type, abort criteria, proof line.

Scopes: CI `TestFault_*` (testcontainers); local compose scenarios before release.

R10. When fault tests are redundant

Do not add fault tests if changes cannot affect distributed steady state.

Pure function, no I/O: No
HTMX/CSS/UI: No
Read-only admin handler: No
Config default, topology unchanged: No
Refactor, same behavior: No (unless races suspected)
Dead code, rename, comments: No
Vendor SDK, no caller change: No
Off-path CLI tools: No
Feature-flagged disabled code: Defer until enabled
New write path, outbox, stream, budget: Yes
New Lua script or shard routing: Yes
Auth, payment, settlement: Yes
Slot migration, autoscaling, fencing: Yes

If financial correctness or ingest SLA can regress, fault is not redundant.

Fault architecture

Ingress (OpenResty) -> Ingestion (gnet) -> Redis shards (Sentinel) -> Processor/Management -> Postgres/ClickHouse.

1 (Ingress :8180): Stale blacklist TTL — Local dict fallback
2 (Tracker :8181-8184): Ring overflow — Bounded MPSC, geo fail-open
3 (Redis :6479-6482): Master kill, NOSCRIPT — Sentinel, breaker, EVAL fallback
4 (Processor/management): Lag, PG txn fail — XAutoClaim, DLQ, `sync_idempotency`
5 (PG/CH): Lock wait, CH parts — Batch writes, janitor, gates

Failure classes (likelihood x severity)

Crash-stop, network partition, resource exhaustion, clock fault, slow dependency, dependency outage, corrupted input, concurrency race, config drift, cascading retry storm.

Single fault per CI run. Compare unaffected shards during outage (control cohort).

